Background. When Lattice renders a deck, its export pipeline embeds
engine assets into the exported artifact: the engine and theme CSS in
exported HTML decks, and the preview/runtime JavaScript inlined into
fluid HTML exports. Lattice also ships engine assets for rendering a
deck with Marp: the Marp kit and the Export-to-Marp bundle. When a
Marp Tool renders a deck to HTML, it embeds the engine CSS into the
rendered deck, and the deck loads the runtime JavaScript from files
beside it. Without this exception, conveying such a deck (for
example, posting it on a web server) could be read as conveying a
covered work, imposing AGPL conditions on the deck's author.

Grant. As a special exception, you may convey the engine assets that
Lattice's export pipeline embeds into a rendered deck — the CSS
bundles, theme CSS, and runtime JavaScript, as embedded by Lattice
itself — as part of that rendered deck, under terms of your choice,
without the AGPL-3.0 applying to the rendered deck as a whole and
without any obligation to offer Corresponding Source for those
embedded assets.

Marp renders. The same permission covers engine assets that Lattice
ships for rendering a deck with a Marp Tool, in the form Lattice
ships them, once a Marp Tool has embedded them into a rendered deck.
It also covers the script and stylesheet files Lattice ships beside
those assets for that deck to load, unmodified, when you convey them
with that deck only so that it displays, and not for any other use.
For this purpose, the embedding a Marp Tool performs, including the
rewriting of CSS selectors to scope them to the deck, counts as the
mechanical embedding of the export pipeline. "Marp Tool" means Marp
CLI or Marp for VS Code, unmodified as published by the Marp project
(https://github.com/marp-team), including the Marp Core and Marpit
they contain, using the rendering engine set by the configuration
Lattice ships with those assets. Marp Core or Marpit used inside any
other program is not a Marp Tool, and neither is any other tool, even
one that embeds the same files the same way.

Limits. This exception applies only to engine assets that Lattice's
own export pipeline embeds into a rendered deck, and to the assets and
files the "Marp renders" paragraph covers. It does not extend to: (a)
the Lattice engine itself or any of its source or object form
distributed other than inside a rendered deck, including the Marp kit
and the Export-to-Marp bundle themselves, except the files the "Marp
renders" paragraph covers; (b) the embedded assets extracted or
separated from a rendered deck for reuse; or (c) any modified version
of the engine or of the embedded assets beyond the mechanical
embedding the export pipeline or a Marp Tool performs. The content of
your deck — your text, images, and data — was never covered by the
AGPL in the first place and needs no exception.

If you convey a rendered deck, you may (but need not) retain this
notice in it. Removing this exception from a copy of Lattice is
permitted by AGPL-3.0 section 7, but copies distributed by Laticent
carry it.
